First, find the slope of the line $2x + 3y = 6$.<br/>i.e., <align}
{/eq}

{eq}3y = 6 - 2x<br/>
{eq}{y=\dfrac{6-2x}{3}}\ \text{Eq(1)}<br/>

Line (1) is in the form {eq}y = mx + c \ {/eq}
So, $$\dfrac{dy}{dx}=-\dfrac{2}{3}<br/>
\\

{/eq}

{eq}m=-\dfrac{2}{3}\\

{/eq}

To find the perpendicular slope:
{eq}-\dfrac{2}{3} \times m=1\\
\\

{eq}\therefore \; m = -\dfrac{3}{2}

{/eq}<br/>

<answer><u>The equation of the line perpendicular to {eq}2x+3y=6{/eq} is {eq}3x+2y=k{/eq}, where k is a constant.</u></answer>

